    <!-- Add insight / key takeaway directly related to activity / learner experience -->
<insight>
        <!-- <title>Key Takeaways: Combining Surds</title> -->
        <p>
            When performing addition and subtraction operations on irrational numbers (surds), keep these two fundamental laws in mind:
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Like Surds:</alert> Only surds with the exact same number under the radical sign can be added or subtracted. When combining them, add or subtract their coefficients while leaving the radicand unchanged:
                        <md>a\sqrt{x} + b\sqrt{x} = (a + b)\sqrt{x}</md>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Unlike Surds:</alert> Surds with different numbers under the radical sign cannot be combined into a single radical term. For example, <m>\sqrt{2} + \sqrt{3} \neq \sqrt{5}</m>. They must remain separate unless converted to an approximate decimal form.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Simplify First:</alert> Always express every radical term in its simplest form (<m>a\sqrt{b}</m>) before checking if they can be added or subtracted as like surds.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
       <p>
        <alert>Common Misconception:</alert> If the numbers inside the radicals are not same, you cannot combine them into a single radical.
        <md>\text{Remember: In general, } \sqrt{a} + \sqrt{b} \neq \sqrt{a + b}</md>
        <!-- <aside><p>Digital resource reference: <c>image_be171e.png</c></p></aside> -->
    </p>
    </insight>
    <!-- <insight>
    </insight> -->
<exploration>
    <title>Adding and Subtracting Irrational Numbers</title>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-adding-irrational-numbers" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="1:1" source="jsxgraph/adding-irrationals.js">
        <slate xml:id="jxgbox" surface="jsxboard" aspect="1:1"/>
        <instructions>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Keep the values of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> (the radicands) equal. Adjust the coefficients <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>. How do the arrows on the number line behave when a value becomes negative?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Change the sliders so that <m>x \neq y</m> (for example, <m>\sqrt{3}</m> and <m>\sqrt{5}</m>). What changes in the simplification text? How does the number line visually represent the addition of these unlike terms?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Click the toggle button to switch the operation from <m>+</m> to <m>-</m>. Describe what happens to the direction of the second arrow (Term 2) on the number line.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Find a combination of coefficients and radicands that results in a total value of exactly <m>0</m>. What must be true about the terms for this to happen?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </instructions>
    </interactive>
</exploration>
    <!-- Add further instruction (not in a block) to develop ideas as needed -->

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- Add at least 3 examples -->

    <example>
        <p>
            <statement>Simplify: <md>8\sqrt{3} + 5\sqrt{3} - 2\sqrt{3}</md></statement>
        </p>
        
      <solution>
        <p>All terms are <q>like surds.</q> We perform the operations on the coefficients: <m>(8 + 5 - 2)\sqrt{3} = 11\sqrt{3}</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
     <p>
        <statement>Simplify: <m>4\sqrt{2} + 7\sqrt{5} + 3\sqrt{2} - 2\sqrt{5}</m></statement>
     </p> 
      <solution>
        <p>Group like surds: <m>(4\sqrt{2} + 3\sqrt{2}) + (7\sqrt{5} - 2\sqrt{5}) = 7\sqrt{2} + 5\sqrt{5}</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>Work out:<md>\sqrt{18} + \sqrt{50}</md></p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                At first glance, these appear to be unlike surds because the radicands <m>18</m> and <m>50</m> are different. We must first simplify each surd into its simplest form <m>a\sqrt{b}</m> by extracting the largest perfect square factors.
            </p>
            <p>
                First, simplify <m>\sqrt{18}</m>:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\sqrt{18} \amp = \sqrt{9 \times 2}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = \sqrt{9} \times \sqrt{2}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = 3\sqrt{2}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Next, simplify <m>\sqrt{50}</m>:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\sqrt{50} \amp = \sqrt{25 \times 2}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = \sqrt{25} \times \sqrt{2}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = 5\sqrt{2}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Now rewrite the original addition problem using the simplified, like surd values and add them together:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\sqrt{18} + \sqrt{50} \amp = 3\sqrt{2} + 5\sqrt{2}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = (3 + 5)\sqrt{2}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = 8\sqrt{2}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
   <example>
        <statement>
            <p>Find the simplest form of <md>2\sqrt{27} - \sqrt{12}</md></p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Simplify both radical terms by checking for perfect square factor components within <m>27</m> and <m>12</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                For the first term, <m>2\sqrt{27}</m>:
                <md>
                    <mrow>2\sqrt{27} \amp = 2\sqrt{9 \times 3}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = 2 \times \sqrt{9} \times \sqrt{3}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = 2 \times 3 \times \sqrt{3}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = 6\sqrt{3}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                For the second term, <m>\sqrt{12}</m>:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\sqrt{12} \amp = \sqrt{4 \times 3}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = \sqrt{4} \times \sqrt{3}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = 2\sqrt{3}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Substitute these simplified expressions back into the subtraction problem:
                <md>
                    <mrow>2\sqrt{27} - \sqrt{12} \amp = 6\sqrt{3} - 2\sqrt{3}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = (6 - 2)\sqrt{3}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = 4\sqrt{3}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>Find the perimeter of a rectangle with length <m>\sqrt{20}</m> cm and width <m>\sqrt{5}</m> cm.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The formula for the perimeter of a rectangle is given by <m>P = 2(\text{length} + \text{width})</m>. Substituting our values gives:
                <md>P = 2(\sqrt{20} + \sqrt{5})</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Before we can add the terms inside the parentheses, we must check if <m>\sqrt{20}</m> can be simplified to a like surd of <m>\sqrt{5}</m>:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\sqrt{20} \amp = \sqrt{4 \times 5}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = \sqrt{4} \times \sqrt{5}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = 2\sqrt{5}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Now substitute <m>2\sqrt{5}</m> back into the perimeter equation, add the like terms within the bracket, and multiply by 2:
                <md>
                    <mrow>P \amp = 2(2\sqrt{5} + 1\sqrt{5})</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = 2((2 + 1)\sqrt{5})</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = 2(3\sqrt{5})</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = (2 \times 3)\sqrt{5}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = 6\sqrt{5}\text{ cm}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
